				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">The morning broke clear and chilly outside Sriani's window. As the bright sun hit her face, she winced and rolled into a sitting position, reaching up to rub the sleepiness from her eyes.  Her bare feet touched the floor and she shivered, the floorboards were as cold as Permafrost! 
<br />

<br />
The room she slept in was virtually empty, all but for two large bags set by the door. Sriani glanced around for a moment, remembering how the room had looked just a short time ago when it was the suite of the Dissonant Intervention guild leader. It had been a place of rest, where all the stress of her day could be left at the door. There, in that corner, had stood a shelf of tomes, for Sriani held a secret love of reading the ancient texts of many lands. Across from that had been a large desk of fine carved wood, where battle plans were sketched in the depths of the night and endless stacks of papers had been signed by her hand.
<br />

<br />
Fighting back a sudden un-warrior like urge to burst into tears, Sriani stood and began her morning stretches. Her lithe, toned body moved fluidly, the muscles rippling under her smooth alabaster skin. Her long red hair, the color of the Flames of Ro, or so her mother had once said, hung behind her in a loose braid. Her face was hard from years of battle.
<br />

<br />
At last she stepped into a tight-fitting pair of leather breeches and a clean white linen shirt that clung close to her body. Over this she began to strap on her armor, which gleamed silver-white in the morning sunshine. It had been polished to a near-impossible shine the night before. From beneath her bed she pulled her weapons, two swords swift and true, the Swiftblade of Zek and the Blackened Acrylia Handblade. Fine weapons earned in her travels across many lands, won from the cold dead hands of her enemies. These she strapped to her back where they could be quickly reached over her shoulder. 
<br />

<br />
Sriani took one last look around the empty room and picked up the two heavy bags, one in each hand.  Without looking back this time, she stepped through the door.
<br />

<br />
The guildhouse of Dissonant Intervention was quiet this morning, hung with an air of tense sadness. Sriani remembered a time when these halls were filled with laughter, song and conversation as the many members of the old guild carried about their lives. Now, since the famine had carried most off either to death or other lands, only a few remained, and these too would soon be leaving the House.
<br />

<br />
As she stepped off the last stair into the Main Hall, she paused for a moment. There were gathered the few remaining members of her once proud force. They looked haggard from starvation and little sleep, but they watched her with eyes full of strength. These were no weak men and women, these were survivors, her people. The sun shone through the windows and bathed them all in a golden glow.
<br />

<br />
No words were spoken. Nothing needed to be said. Everyone gathered here knew this day had been coming for a long time, and speaking of it would only make the pain more sharp.  Sriani met the eyes of each individual, holding their gaze for a moment as silent goodbyes passed between them. Some nodded slightly, in acknowledgment or thanks. Again, that very un-warriorlike feeling choked her throat, and she knew she would not be able to speak even had she wished to. She straightened her back, threw back her head and began to walk towards the main door.
<br />

<br />
Near the door, hanging back from the others was a man. Tall, muscular and clad in armor of platinum steel, his blonde almost-white hair pulled back in a tail, Chorun watched her with an expression as steely and hard as the armor he wore. Sriani stared him down, damned if the man would make her blush or hesitate on this of all days. He watched her with an almost sneer on his lips. The man was a paladin, the second-in-command of the guild, and her husband.
<br />

<br />
It had been a hard day when he had told her that he would not leave. Stubborn to a fault, he refused to leave the guild that had protected and strengthened him from a little boy. Even though the hall stood near-empty, surrounded by fallow fields, the stables and barns quiet, Chorun would stay, and try to rebuild Dissonant Intervention to its former glory. He claimed he understood her reasons for leaving. The chance to join one of the largest, most successful guilds in all the land was one no self-respecting guild-member would pass up. Yet, he would.
<br />

<br />
She dropped her eyes from his and kept walking. She was almost out now, she could see the road ahead of her, dusty from little rain and stretching far out to the horizon, where in the distance the Butcherblock Mountains stood wreathed in clouds. There waited her new future, her destiny with the Covenant of the Wolf. She set one booted foot down into the dirt, and then the other. 
<br />

<br />
Sriani felt Chorun's cold stare burning into her back and a hitch in her chest promised that once she was sufficiently alone, she finally would cry. Memories of the past few years played in her mind like a torturous dream, her friends and companions lost to the terrible famine or fled to find safety. She only hoped that Covenant would accept her, take her in, and give her a new home, for now, she had none.
<br />

<br />
To be continued...<div style="vertical-align: bottom"><br /><br /><br />_________________<br /><a href="http://www.magelo.com/eq_view_profile.html?num=956867" target="_blank" class="postlink">~Sriani HeartofWolf~</a>
<br />
66 Lady of War
