			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">&lt;&lt;&lt;For the next installment in my tale, I have taken some liberties with actual events in-game and changed them to fit my story. I hope no one minds. *flashes her poetic license* hehe&gt;&gt;&gt;
<br />

<br />
After a few weeks in her new guildhouse, Sriani was beginning to feel at home. She took part in the daily chores like everyone else did, and attended the trainings held by Master Adaniel and Baron Neveah. In the evenings she enjoyed listening to Soeva play the lute, while Nhacsii sang, or Tabriel opened a book of histories and read aloud. She was beginning to feel as part of the family.
<br />

<br />
One day not too long after her arrival, the enchanter who was head of Recruiting, Kobil, approached her as she was finishing a training with Adaniel.  
<br />

<br />
"Good day Kobil." She smiled to him, shaking his hand. He nodded to her and gently drew her aside.
<br />

<br />
"Sriani, I need you to come to the gate house with me. There is something you must see." His voice was worried, and it immedietly raised her concern. She hastily wiped off, apologizing to Adaniel for leaving early (he assured her he completely understood), and followed Kobil across the compound to the gatehouse.
<br />

<br />
Inside the dim room was Tabriel with her ever-present stack of papers, and before her, seated on the floor in various states of exhaustion, were four men. They were dirty and travel-worn, bearing the wounds of battle and the tell-tale signs of malnutrition. Tabriel glanced up at Kobil and Sriani's entrance, and bit her lip.
<br />

<br />
"Thank the Gods, Kobil. I don't have any idea what to do with them." She sighed, stepping back. Kobil nodded.
<br />

<br />
"It's allright child." He soothed her and she glanced at Sriani, offering a tight smile. Sriani barely noticed, she was staring at the men on the floor.
<br />

<br />
One was an Iksar Necromancer, his robes in tatters. Another was a Dark Elf Shadowknight, his armor dulled by road-dust. The next was a Vah Shir Beastlord, his fur matted, his warder panting beside him. And the last...oh the last...
<br />

<br />
"Chorun." She breathed softly.  He looked up, hearing his name, and stared at her dully. She bit her lip to keep the tears away. "You came."
<br />

<br />
"Yes." He answered simply, and struggled to stand. "The guild is gone. The house burned down a few weeks ago, shortly after you left. I brought everyone out safe, but some chose to go their own way. I brought as many as I could with me."
<br />

<br />
"Oh gods..." She moaned. Her guild house...burned? Her friends and companions...gone or maybe dead? It was too much to take in so soon. 
<br />

<br />
Chorun watched her with an angry set to his jaw. 
<br />

<br />
"I brought them here because there is no safe refuge anymore. The lands are overriden with the Curse. The other guild houses would not take us in. I come to you..." He looked to Kobil and stared him down hard, man to man. "Because I have no where else to go. And I beg your mercy."
<br />

<br />
Sriani knew how much this cost him to say. His pride must be shattered for him to beg mercy of any man. Kobil glanced away and at her.
<br />

<br />
"This is outside our normal guidelines for recruitment. We are not a safe house..." He coughed quickly and went on. "But Sriani here has been a great asset to us, and I trust her judgement."
<br />

<br />
"No." Sriani said suddenly. Everyone turned to stare at her.
<br />

<br />
"Do not judge these men on my word. Let them prove themselves." She said decisively. Chorun glanced at her, and for a moment she thought she saw love again in his eyes, but then it was gone quickly, replaced by stoney hardness. Kobil nodded slowly.
<br />

<br />
"As you say, but I will not allow them to remain on the roads at the mercy of the Curse. You shall all four be allowed into our ranks as recruits, just like any other applicant, you shall prove yourselves on the basis of your own deeds." 
<br />

<br />
Chorun nodded, glancing at his companions who nodded also. Tabriel sighed good-naturedly.
<br />

<br />
"Four of you at once! Well, here you go, fill these out." She began thrusting papers at them as they stood and filled a line. Sriani opened the door and stepped into the bright sunlight, the sound of Tab's instructions fading out as the door closed behind her. She hugged herself, staring off into the sky.
<br />

<br />
"I made you a member of my recruit council because I trust your judgement." Kobil spoke softly behind her. "Though some questioned my decision, as you are so new to us, I see now I was right in it."
<br />

<br />
He touched her shoulder briefly, a gesture of comfort between comrades, and then he walked off across the courtyard to the main hall. She stood alone for along while, until the door opened behind her and she turned to watch the four men stream out.
<br />

<br />
"Igelei, Zefnir..Shavhir!" She smiled, glancing at each of them in turn. They smiled at her, but their smiles were guarded, and she understood at once that she was no longer the trusted guild leader and friend she had been. She was one to be wary of, a traitor, who had fled at the first sign of trouble. She gritted her teeth, determined to prove herself once again to her friends. "I'm glad you are all here."
<br />

<br />
Behind them, Chorun emerged, and their eyes met over their friend's heads. Igelei gave a short little laugh.
<br />

<br />
"C'mon guys, lets let these two alone." He slung his pack over his shoulder and the three men moved off to the barracks.
<br />

<br />
"Husband." She whispered, moving towards him. He took a step back and glanced away, then headed off to the Paladin building. She watched him go, tears stinging her eyes, and knew that of all of them, his friendship and love would be the hardest to win back.
<br />

<br />
Behind her, Tabriel opened the door and stepped out, taking in the scene.
<br />

<br />
"Men are like trees, dear, they will not bend, you must cut them down." She laughed. "They see things in black and white only, you must be harsh and steady with him as he does not expect you to be. He will see the mistake he has made in turning from you."
<br />

<br />
Sriani sighed. "I wonder if he will. Or was it my mistake in turning from him?"
<br />

<br />
"Only the Gods know, dear one. And they leave it up to us to unravel while they sit in their thrones and laugh." Tabriel walked off towards the main hall, leaving Sriani alone with her thoughts. So it was going to be a challenge then? Well, she was up to a challenge, and Chorun was in for a surprise if he thought for a minute she was going to back down.
<br />

<br />
